Let's ground it in a simple situation. A selection structure lets the program choose between paths based on a condition. In C, that appears with `if`, `else if`, and `else`, which do not repeat; they only choose which block to run. ```c
if (grade >= 70) {
    printf("Pass\n");
} else {
    printf("Fail\n");
}
``` Would it help more to see a tiny C example or a plain-language analogy?

It helps to separate the concept from C. A `do while` loop guarantees that the block runs at least once before checking the condition. The key difference compared with `while` is when the condition gets evaluated. ```c
do {
    scanf("%d", &option);
} while (option < 0);
``` Do you want to keep it language-agnostic first or move to C now?

Focus on what the program is trying to achieve first. Stopping a loop correctly means designing a condition or exit signal that matches the goal of the problem. The loop exit should not be a random number but a rule that actually matches the task. ```c
while (pending > 0) {
    pending--;
}
``` Would it help more to see a tiny C example or a plain-language analogy?

